      "writtenOn": "2026-11-19T21:24:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "SRFI 146\u0027s comparator restrictions require a hash function only \"unless the equality predicate of the comparator is eq?, eqv?, equal?, string\u003d?, or string-ci\u003d?\". So `(hashmap-ref (hashmap (make-comparator string? string\u003d? #f #f) \"a\" 1 \"b\" 2) \"b\")` should give 2, but it raises \"the comparator of a hashmap needs a hash function\", as does `(make-comparator #t equal? #f #f)`. The regression test asserts that at line 527. When the comparator can\u0027t hash, make the table from its equality predicate (`(make-hash-table equality)`; 125 supplies the hash for those five), keep the comparator in the record, and have the hashmap comparator use `hash-table-hash-function`. The same applies to 113 (113.sld:153).",
      "revId": "3238d22f6af70e356c32dad7fc3ac652b3a7627b",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
